__d(function(e,t,i,r,o,a,c){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:12,chapter_title:"Electricity and Circuits",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is an electric circuit? What are its essential components?",back:"An electric circuit is a closed path through which electric current flows. Essential components: (1) A source of electricity \u2014 cell or battery (e.g. a torch cell, AA battery); (2) Conducting wires \u2014 to connect components and allow current to flow; (3) A load/device \u2014 something that uses electricity (bulb, motor, buzzer); (4) A switch \u2014 to open or close the circuit. The circuit must be CLOSED (complete/unbroken) for current to flow. An open circuit (broken path) stops all current flow.",memory_hook:"CIRCUIT = CIRCLE. Like a running track \u2014 the runner (current) can only run if the track is complete (closed). Break the track \u2192 runner stops. Break the circuit \u2192 current stops."},{id:"fc02",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a cell? What are its two terminals?",back:"A cell is a device that converts chemical energy into electrical energy, providing the push (voltage) that drives electric current through a circuit. A cell has TWO terminals: (1) Positive terminal (+) \u2014 the raised bump end (marked with a + sign on the cell); (2) Negative terminal (\u2212) \u2014 the flat end (marked with a \u2212 sign). In a circuit, current flows from the positive terminal through the circuit and back to the negative terminal. Common examples: AA cell (pencil cell), AAA cell, torch cell (D cell), button cell (used in watches).",memory_hook:"Cell = ELECTRICITY PUMP. + terminal = pipe inlet (current starts here). \u2212 terminal = pipe outlet (current returns here). Positive = pointy/raised end. Negative = flat end."},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a battery? How is it different from a cell?",back:"A battery is a combination of two or more cells connected together to produce a higher voltage. A single cell (like a AA cell) produces 1.5 volts. A battery of two cells produces 3 volts. In a battery, cells are typically connected positive terminal to negative terminal (series connection). In everyday language, people often call a single cell a 'battery,' but scientifically, a battery has multiple cells. Examples: a 9V smoke detector battery (contains 6 cells \xd7 1.5V); a car battery (12V = 6 lead-acid cells); a mobile phone battery.",memory_hook:"BATTERY = MULTIPLE CELLS TEAM. One cell = 1 player. Battery = team of players. More cells = more voltage = more push for the current."},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a switch? What happens to a circuit when a switch is open vs. closed?",back:"A switch is a device that can open or close an electric circuit. When the switch is CLOSED (ON): the circuit is complete; current flows; the bulb glows (or device works). When the switch is OPEN (OFF): the circuit is broken; no current flows; the bulb does not glow. A switch works by physically connecting or disconnecting the conducting path. Types: toggle switch (light switch on wall), push-button switch, rocker switch. A switch in the open position creates a gap in the circuit \u2014 the same effect as removing a wire.",memory_hook:"SWITCH = CIRCUIT GATEKEEPER. Closed gate = current passes (bulb ON). Open gate = current blocked (bulb OFF). Think of a switch as a drawbridge: down (closed) = cars cross (current flows); up (open) = cars stopped (no current)."},{id:"fc05",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is an electric current? In which direction does it flow in a circuit?",back:"Electric current is the flow of electric charge through a conductor. It is the movement of electrons through a wire. By convention (historical agreement), current is said to flow from the positive terminal (+) of the cell, through the circuit (external path), to the negative terminal (\u2212). However, electrons actually flow in the opposite direction (from \u2212 to +). For school purposes: conventional current flows from + to \u2212. The unit of electric current is the Ampere (A). Current flows ONLY in a closed (complete) circuit \u2014 an open circuit has zero current.",memory_hook:"Current = FLOW OF CHARGE. Water flows in a pipe; current flows in a wire. Convention: + to \u2212 externally (like water from high tank to low). Electrons flow the other way, but we use conventional direction at this level."},{id:"fc06",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the difference between a conductor and an insulator? Give two examples of each.",back:"A conductor is a material that allows electric current to flow through it easily. Examples: copper (most common wiring material), aluminium, iron, graphite (pencil lead), salt water, human body. A conductor has free electrons that can move easily. An insulator is a material that does not allow electric current to flow through it. Examples: rubber, plastic, glass, wood, dry air, pure water, paper. Insulators have tightly bound electrons that cannot move. Practical use: electrical wires are made of copper (conductor) covered with plastic/rubber (insulator) for safety.",memory_hook:"CONDUCTOR = CURRENT'S FRIEND (lets current pass). INSULATOR = CURRENT'S ENEMY (blocks current). Copper wire = conductor inside, rubber coat = insulator outside. Safety by design."},{id:"fc07",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How can you use a simple circuit with a bulb and cell to test if an object is a conductor or insulator?",back:"A circuit tester for conductors/insulators: make a simple circuit with a cell, a bulb, and two free wire ends (the 'tester probes'). Touch the two probe ends to the object being tested. If the bulb GLOWS \u2192 the object completes the circuit \u2192 it is a CONDUCTOR (allows current to pass). If the bulb DOES NOT GLOW \u2192 the circuit is incomplete \u2192 the object is an INSULATOR (does not allow current). This simple test is used to check if materials like iron nails (conductor), rubber eraser (insulator), pencil graphite (conductor), plastic scale (insulator), and copper coin (conductor) allow current.",memory_hook:"BULB GLOWS = CONDUCTOR. NO GLOW = INSULATOR. The bulb is the truth-teller. Current passes? Yes \u2192 conductor, bulb glows. No current? Insulator, darkness. Simple."},{id:"fc08",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Draw and label the circuit symbols for: cell, battery, bulb, switch (open), switch (closed), wire.",back:"Circuit symbols (standardised): Cell: two vertical lines \u2014 short thick line (\u2212) and long thin line (+), separated by a gap. Battery: multiple cell symbols in a row. Bulb: a circle with an X inside (representing the filament). Switch (open): a gap in the wire with a small line (lever) not touching \u2014 represents an open/off switch. Switch (closed): the lever touching the circuit \u2014 represents closed/on switch. Wire: a straight line. These symbols are international standards used in circuit diagrams (schematics) so that any engineer or student anywhere can read and draw circuits.",memory_hook:"Cell = two lines (short \u2212 and long +). Bulb = circle with X. Open switch = broken line with a lifted lever. Closed switch = completed line. Learn the symbols like learning the alphabet of circuits."},{id:"fc09",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why does a bulb not glow if the circuit is incomplete (open)?",back:"A bulb glows because electric current passes through its thin filament wire, which heats up and emits light (this is called incandescence). For current to flow, there must be a complete, unbroken path (closed circuit) from the cell's positive terminal through the bulb and back to the negative terminal. If the circuit is open (broken wire, open switch, disconnected component), no current flows through the filament. Without current, no heating, no incandescence, no light. Even a tiny break (gap of air, which is an insulator) in the circuit path stops all current flow.",memory_hook:"OPEN CIRCUIT = DEAD END. Current needs a complete loop \u2014 like a racetrack. One break in the track \u2192 all cars stop. Break the circuit \u2192 current stops \u2192 bulb stays dark."},{id:"fc10",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why are electrical wires covered with plastic or rubber? Why not leave them bare?",back:"Electrical wires are made of copper (a conductor) inside, covered with plastic or rubber (insulators) outside for two critical reasons: (1) Safety \u2014 if bare copper wires were touched accidentally, current would flow through the human body (which is a conductor), causing electric shock, injury, or death. The plastic/rubber insulating layer prevents accidental contact with live conductors; (2) Preventing short circuits \u2014 bare wires touching each other creates a short circuit (current bypasses the intended path), which can cause fire and damage. The insulating layer keeps wires electrically isolated from each other and from the environment.",memory_hook:"WIRE = COPPER INSIDE, PLASTIC ARMOUR OUTSIDE. The copper carries current; the plastic protects you from the current. Like a sword in a sheath \u2014 the blade (conductor) is safely enclosed."},{id:"fc11",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a short circuit? Why is it dangerous?",back:"A short circuit occurs when the positive and negative terminals of a cell or battery are connected directly by a conductor (wire) without any resistance/load in between. In a short circuit: an extremely large current flows; the cell discharges very rapidly; the wire and cell heat up dangerously; fire or explosion can occur. Example: connecting a wire directly from the + to the \u2212 of a cell without a bulb in between \u2014 the wire will heat up. Short circuits in household wiring cause electrical fires. Fuses and circuit breakers are safety devices that break the circuit when current becomes dangerously high.",memory_hook:"SHORT CIRCUIT = EXPRESSWAY FOR CURRENT. Normal circuit has a load (speed bumps). Short circuit has no load \u2192 current goes at full speed \u2192 wire melts from heat. Always include a load in your circuit!"},{id:"fc12",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is an electric fuse? Why is it used?",back:"A fuse is a safety device in an electric circuit made of a thin wire of low melting point metal (usually tin-lead alloy). When excessive current flows (due to a short circuit or overload), the fuse wire heats up and MELTS, breaking the circuit and stopping the current before it can damage appliances or cause fire. A fuse is a sacrificial device \u2014 it deliberately burns out to protect the rest of the circuit. Modern homes use MCBs (Miniature Circuit Breakers) \u2014 which trip (switch off) automatically when excess current flows and can be reset (unlike a fuse which must be replaced).",memory_hook:"FUSE = CIRCUIT SELF-DESTRUCT HERO. It sacrifices itself (melts) to save the rest of the circuit. The fuse is the hero that takes the hit so your TV and refrigerator don't get damaged."},{id:"fc13",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Is graphite (pencil lead) a conductor or an insulator? How could you test this?",back:"Graphite (pencil lead/carbon) is a CONDUCTOR. Unlike most non-metals, graphite has free electrons (due to its layered structure) that allow it to conduct electricity. Test: set up a circuit with a cell, bulb, and two free probe wires. Touch the probes to the sharpened graphite tip of a pencil. The bulb will glow (dimly, since graphite has higher resistance than metals). This is a surprising result because graphite looks like a soft, dark solid but behaves electrically like a metal. This is why graphite is used in electrodes for batteries and electrolysis.",memory_hook:"PENCIL = SURPRISE CONDUCTOR. The 'lead' in a pencil is actually graphite \u2014 a conductor. Only metals conduct? Wrong \u2014 graphite proves non-metals can too. Surprise your friends: a pencil can complete a circuit!"},{id:"fc14",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What do these abbreviations stand for in electricity: AC, DC, V, A?",back:"AC = Alternating Current \u2014 current that reverses direction periodically. Household electricity is AC (in India: 230V, 50Hz). DC = Direct Current \u2014 current that flows in only one direction. Cells and batteries produce DC. V = Volt \u2014 unit of voltage (electrical pressure/push). A single cell provides 1.5V. A = Ampere \u2014 unit of electric current (flow rate of charge). A household circuit carries 5\u201315A. At Grade 6 level, focus on DC circuits using cells. AC is used in homes and is dangerous at household voltages \u2014 always use cells/batteries for experiments.",memory_hook:"AC = Alternates (back and forth \u2014 like a pendulum). DC = Direct (one-way \u2014 like a river). Volt = Voltage (V). Ampere = current (A). Homes use AC. Torch uses DC. Remember: AC is what comes from the wall socket."},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name four safety rules to follow when working with electricity at home.",back:"Electrical safety rules: (1) Never touch electrical switches or appliances with wet hands \u2014 water conducts electricity and causes shock; (2) Do not overload sockets with too many appliances \u2014 causes overheating and fire; (3) Never play with electric wires \u2014 frayed wires have exposed conductors; (4) Always use a licensed electrician for repairs \u2014 do not DIY high-voltage work; (5) Ensure a main switch/MCB is available and accessible; (6) Keep electrical appliances away from water; (7) Do not insert metal objects into sockets. For school experiments: ONLY use cells and batteries \u2014 NEVER experiment with household AC current.",memory_hook:"WET + ELECTRIC = DANGER. Keep water and electricity apart. Use only cells for experiments. Remember: household current (230V AC) can kill \u2014 cells (1.5V DC) are safe for learning."},{id:"fc16",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the difference between a series circuit and a parallel circuit?",back:"Series circuit: all components are connected in a single loop, one after another. Current flows through each component sequentially. If one bulb fuses, all bulbs go off (the chain is broken). Example: old-style Christmas lights. Parallel circuit: components are connected in separate branches between the same two points. Each component gets the full supply voltage. If one bulb fuses, the others continue working (each branch is independent). Example: house wiring \u2014 switching off one room's light does not affect other rooms. Household circuits use parallel connections so each appliance works independently.",memory_hook:"SERIES = ONE LANE ROAD. Blockage = all traffic stops. PARALLEL = MULTI-LANE HIGHWAY. One lane blocked \u2192 other lanes still flow. Home wiring must be parallel so one off switch doesn't darken the whole house."},{id:"fc17",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why does touching a live wire give you an electric shock?",back:"The human body is a conductor (it contains water and dissolved minerals/salts that carry ions). When you touch a live wire (carrying high-voltage current) without insulation, your body completes the circuit \u2014 current flows from the wire, through your body, and to the ground. This current passing through the body disrupts the electrical signals that control heart and muscles, causing muscle spasms, burns, cardiac arrest, and death in severe cases. Even household voltage (230V AC in India) can be fatal. This is why electrical safety \u2014 insulated wires, rubber footwear, not touching with wet hands \u2014 is critical. Cells (1.5V) are safe for school experiments because the voltage is too low to push current through the body's resistance.",memory_hook:"BODY = WET CONDUCTOR. Touch live wire \u2192 you complete the circuit \u2192 current uses your body as a path. 230V through your body = medical emergency. 1.5V cell = safe for experiments. Voltage matters: low = safe, high = deadly."},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"In Karnataka, from where does the electricity in your home come? Trace the path from source to your light bulb.",back:"Karnataka's electricity path: (1) Generation \u2014 power plants generate electricity: hydroelectric (Sharavathi, Kalinadi, Tungabhadra dams), thermal (Raichur Thermal Power Station), solar (Pavagada), wind (Chitradurga, Gadag), and nuclear (Kaiga, Uttara Kannada). KPCL (Karnataka Power Corporation Limited) manages generation. (2) Transmission \u2014 high-voltage electricity (220,000V) is transmitted via tall transmission towers across long distances (KPTCL manages this). (3) Distribution \u2014 voltage is stepped down by substations to 11,000V, then to 415V, then to 230V for homes. BESCOM distributes in Bengaluru. (4) Consumption \u2014 230V AC reaches your home's meter, then your switchboard, then your light switch, and finally your bulb.",memory_hook:"GTDC: Generate (dam/solar) \u2192 Transmit (high voltage towers) \u2192 Distribute (step down) \u2192 Consume (your bulb). Four steps from river to your room in Karnataka."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Classify these as conductors or insulators: copper wire, rubber eraser, iron nail, glass, salt water, dry wood, graphite, plastic scale.",back:"Conductors (allow current): copper wire (excellent conductor \u2014 used in wiring), iron nail (metal conductor), salt water (ionic conductor \u2014 dissolved salts carry charge), graphite (carbon conductor). Insulators (block current): rubber eraser (rubber = insulator \u2014 used to coat wires), glass (insulator \u2014 used in electrical fittings), dry wood (insulator \u2014 however wet wood conducts), plastic scale (plastic = insulator). Remember: most metals = conductors. Most non-metals = insulators. Exceptions: graphite (non-metal conductor), salt water (liquid conductor).",memory_hook:"METALS CONDUCT (copper, iron, aluminium, silver, gold). NON-METALS INSULATE (plastic, rubber, glass, wood). EXCEPTION: graphite conducts despite being a non-metal. Test with bulb-and-cell circuit to be sure."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A child connects a cell, a bulb, and a switch in a circuit. The bulb does not glow even when the switch is closed. List four possible reasons.",back:"Possible reasons the bulb does not glow (with switch closed): (1) The cell is used up (dead/discharged) \u2014 no longer has chemical energy to drive current; (2) The bulb is fused (the filament inside is broken) \u2014 the filament wire has snapped; (3) A wire connection is loose or disconnected \u2014 even one broken connection opens the circuit; (4) The switch connections are not making proper contact even when closed; (5) The cell terminals are connected incorrectly (+ to + or \u2212 to \u2212); (6) A wire is broken internally (invisible break). Troubleshooting: replace the cell, replace the bulb, check all connections, ensure correct polarity.",memory_hook:"CIRCUIT DETECTIVES CHECK: (1) Is the cell alive? (2) Is the bulb alive? (3) Are all connections tight? (4) Is the switch actually making contact? Four suspects when the bulb won't glow. Eliminate one at a time."}]}},2907,[]);